'''broad''' (brâd), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''broode, brode,'' < AS. ''brāde'' (= MHG. ''breite,'' G. ''breit''), broadly; from the adj.] '''1†.''' Broadly; openly; plainly.
{{smaller block|Crist spak himself ful ''broode'' in holy writ.
{{right|''Chaucer,'' Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 739.}}}}
'''2†.''' Widely; copiously; abundantly. ''Chaucer.'' —'''3.''' Broadly; fully.
{{smaller block|With all his crimes ''broad'' blown, as flush as May.
{{right|''Shak.,'' Hamlet, iii. 3.}}
Lying ''broad'' awake I thought of you and Effie dear.
{{right|''Tennyson,'' May Queen (Conclusion).}}}}

'''broad†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''broden,'' < AS. ''brādian,'' spread, < ''brād,'' broad. Cf. ''bread''<sup>2</sup>.] To make broad; spread.
{{smaller block|Tyll the blessed bredd [bird] ''brodid'' his wingis.
{{right|''Richard the Redeless.''}}}}

'''broad-arrow''' (brâd′ar″ō), ''n.'' [< ME. ''brode arow, brodarwe,'' etc., a heavy arrow; < ''broad'' + ''arrow.'']
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Broad-arrow.}}}}
The royal mark of British government stores of every description, which it is felony to obliterate or deface. {{smaller|Persons unlawfully in possession of goods marked with the broad-arrow forfeit the goods and are subject to a penalty of £200. The ''broad-arrow'' was the cognizance of Henry, Viscount Sydney, Earl of Romney, Master-general of Ordnance from 1693 to 1702, and was first used in his time. In heraldry it differs from the pheon (which see) in having the inside of the barbs plain.}}

'''broadax''' (brâd′aks), ''n.'' [< ME. ''brodax, broodaxe,'' etc., < AS. ''brādæx,'' < ''brād,'' broad, + ''æx,'' ax: see ''broad'' and ''ax''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1†.''' A battle-ax.—'''2.''' An ax with a broad edge, for hewing timber.

readings.c2s3:
